window.HW_IMG={"lrv15":{"id":"as15-86-11603","title":"Astronaut James Irwin works at Lunar Roving Vehicle during Apollo 15 EVA","date":"1971-07-31","center":"JSC","photographer":"","desc":"AS15-86-11603 (31 July 1971) --- Astronaut James B. Irwin, lunar module pilot, works at the Lunar Roving Vehicle during the first Apollo 15 lunar surface extravehicular activity (EVA) at the Hadley-Apennine landing site. The shadow of the Lunar Module \"Falcon\" is in the foreground. This view is looking northeast, with Mount Hadley in the background. This photograph was taken by astronaut David R. Scott, commander.","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/as15-86-11603/as15-86-11603~large.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/as15-86-11603","path":"hw/img/lrv15.jpg"},"alsep12":{"id":"as12-47-6919","title":"Astronaut Alan Bean deploys ALSEP during first Apollo 12 EVA on moon","date":"1969-11-19","center":"JSC","photographer":"","desc":"AS12-47-6919 (19 Nov. 1969) --- Astronaut Alan L. Bean, lunar module pilot, deploys components of the Apollo Lunar Surface Experiments Package (ALSEP) during the first Apollo 12 extravehicular activity (EVA) on the moon. The photo was made by astronaut Charles Conrad Jr., commander, using a 70mm handheld Hasselblad camera modified for lunar surface usage.","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/as12-47-6919/as12-47-6919~large.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/as12-47-6919","path":"hw/img/alsep12.jpg"},"m20_hw":{"id":"PIA24333","title":"Perseverance and Mars 2020 Spacecraft Components on the Surface","date":"2021-02-22","center":"JPL","photographer":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona","desc":"This first image of NASA's Perseverance Rover on the surface of Mars from the High Resolution Imaging Experiment (HiRISE) camera aboard NASA's Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter (MRO) shows the many parts of the Mars 2020 mission landing system that got the rover safely on the ground. The image was taken on Feb. 19, 2021.  The rover itself sits at the center of a blast pattern created by the hovering descent stage that lowered it there using the sky crane maneuver. The descent stage flew off to crash at a safe distance, creating a V-shaped debris pattern that points back toward the rover. Earlier in ","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA24333/PIA24333~large.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA24333","path":"hw/img/m20_hw.jpg"},"msl_orbit":{"id":"PIA15696","title":"Orbiter View of Curiosity From Nearly Straight Overhead","date":"2012-08-31","center":"JPL","photographer":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/Univ. of Arizona/USGS","desc":"Details such as the shadow of the mast on NASA Mars rover Curiosity appear in an image taken Aug. 17, 2012, by the HiRISE camera on NASA Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter, from more directly overhead than previous HiRISE images of Curiosity.","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA15696/PIA15696~orig.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA15696","path":"hw/img/msl_orbit.jpg"},"nsyt_hw":{"id":"PIA22877","title":"Locations of InSight, its Heat Shield and its Parachute","date":"2018-12-13","center":"JPL","photographer":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona","desc":"An annotated image of the surface of Mars, taken by the HiRISE camera on NASA's Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter (MRO) on May 30, 2014. The annotations - added after InSight landed on Nov. 26, 2018 - display the locations of NASA's InSight lander, its heat shield and parachute.  https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA22877","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA22877/PIA22877~orig.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA22877","path":"hw/img/nsyt_hw.jpg"},"phx_orbit":{"id":"PIA22223","title":"NASA's Phoenix Lander on Mars, Nearly a Decade Later","date":"2018-02-20","center":"JPL","photographer":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/Univ. of Arizona","desc":"This is one of two images taken nearly a decade apart of NASA's Mars Phoenix Lander and related hardware around the mission's May 25, 2008, landing site on far-northern Mars. By late 2017, dust had obscured much of what was visible two months after the landing.  Both images were taken by the High Resolution Imaging Science Experiment (HiRISE) camera on NASA's Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter. The one with three patches of darker ground -- where landing events removed dust -- was taken on July 20, 2008. It is Fig. 1, an excerpt of HiRISE observation PSP_009290_2485. The one with a more even coating ","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA22223/PIA22223~orig.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA22223","path":"hw/img/phx_orbit.jpg"},"merb_hw":{"id":"PIA21494","title":"Rover Landing Hardware at Eagle Crater, Mars","date":"2017-04-21","center":"JPL","photographer":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/Univ. of Arizona","desc":"The bright landing platform left behind by NASA's Mars Exploration Rover Opportunity in 2004 is visible inside Eagle Crater, at upper right in this April 8, 2017, observation by NASA's Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter.  Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter arrived at Mars in March 2006, more than two years after Opportunity's landing on Jan. 25, 2004, Universal Time (Jan. 24, PDT). This is the first image of Eagle Crater from the orbiter's High Resolution Imaging Science Experiment (HiRISE) camera, which has optics that include the most powerful telescope ever sent to Mars.  Eagle Crater is about 72 feet (2","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA21494/PIA21494~large.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA21494","path":"hw/img/merb_hw.jpg"},"edm_impact":{"id":"PIA21132","title":"Schiaparelli Impact Site on Mars, in Color","date":"2016-11-03","center":"JPL","photographer":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/Univ. of Arizona","desc":"On Nov. 1, 2016, the High Resolution Imaging Science Experiment (HiRISE) camera on NASA's Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter observed the impact site of Europe's Schiaparelli test lander, gaining the first color view of the site since the lander's Oct. 19, 2016, arrival.  These cutouts from the observation cover three locations where parts of the spacecraft reached the ground: the lander module itself in the upper portion, the parachute and back shell at lower left, and the heat shield at lower right. The heat shield location was outside of the area covered in color. The scale bar of 10 meters (32.8 ","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA21132/PIA21132~orig.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA21132","path":"hw/img/edm_impact.jpg"},"zr_orbit":{"id":"PIA24914","title":"The Zhurong Rover Explores Utopia Planitia","date":"2021-09-29","center":"JPL","photographer":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona","desc":"The Chinese Zhurong rover, named after the god of fire in Chinese mythology, landed in Utopia Planitia in May 2021. Utopia Planitia is a volcanic region located in the low-lying Northern Hemisphere of Mars.  The expansive, flat, smooth plains of Utopia are dimpled by impact craters, volcanic domes, light-toned ridges made of wind-blown materials, and troughs. If the rover continues to drive south from its landing site, this HiRISE view shows the kind of terrain and features Zhurong may explore.  https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA24914","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA24914/PIA24914~large.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA24914","path":"hw/img/zr_orbit.jpg"},"heli_delta":{"id":"PIA25080","title":"Ingenuity Flights to the Delta","date":"2022-03-15","center":"JPL","photographer":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona/USGS","desc":"This annotated overhead image from NASA's Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter (MRO) depicts the multiple flights – and two different routes – the agency's Ingenuity Mars Helicopter could take on its way to Jezero Crater's delta. The location of Ingenuity as of March 14, 2022, is indicated by the red dot. This map is made using images from MRO's High Resolution Imaging Experiment (HiRISE) camera.  The first flight in this series (indicated by the number 1 in blue) occurred on March 10, 2022. After the next flight – which includes a sharp bend in the course to avoid a large hill – the helicopter team wi","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA25080/PIA25080~orig.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA25080","path":"hw/img/heli_delta.jpg"},"bea_orbit":{"id":"PIA19107","title":"Beagle 2 Lander on Mars, With Panels Deployed","date":"2015-01-16","center":"JPL","photographer":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/Univ. of Arizona/University of Leicester","desc":"A configuration interpreted as the United Kingdom Beagle 2 Lander, with solar panels at least partially deployed, is indicated in this composite of two images from NASA Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter.","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA19107/PIA19107~orig.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA19107","path":"hw/img/bea_orbit.jpg"},"lro_a16_s4b":{"id":"GSFC_20171208_Archive_e000501","title":"LRO Finds Apollo 16 Booster Rocket Impact Site","date":"2017-12-08","center":"GSFC","photographer":"","desc":"After decades of uncertainty, the Apollo 16 S-IVB impact site on the lunar surface has been identified. S-IVBs were portions of the Saturn V rockets that brought astronauts to the moon. The site was identified in imagery from the high-resolution LROC Narrow Angle Camera aboard NASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter.  Beginning with Apollo 13, the S-IVB rocket stages were deliberately impacted on the lunar surface after they were used. Seismometers placed on the moon by earlier Apollo astronauts measured the energy of these impacts to shed light on the internal lunar structure. Locations of the cr","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e000501/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e000501~orig.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e000501","path":"hw/img/lro_a16_s4b.jpg"},"msl_ellipse":{"id":"PIA18399","title":"Curiosity Mars Rover Reaching Edge of Its Landing Ellipse","date":"2014-07-08","center":"JPL","photographer":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/Univ. of Arizona","desc":"This June 27, 2014, image from the HiRISE camera on NASA Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter shows NASA Curiosity Mars rover on the rover landing-ellipse boundary, which is superimposed on the image.","src":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/PIA18399/PIA18399~orig.jpg","page":"https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA18399","path":"hw/img/msl_ellipse.jpg"}};

